    summary: "Design-led growth for Moor Body Care: a beauty & skincare overhaul pairing creative redesigns with performance work — faster pages, clearer paths, higher order value.",
    challenge: "Moor Body Care's buyers don't tolerate guesswork: a moor therapy skincare purchase is researched across multiple visits, on whatever device is at hand. The audit had to confirm the storefront keeps its promises consistently — pricing, availability, delivery — across every repeat visit and viewport, and that the checkout path stays intact from first click to confirmation. Nothing was guesswork: each finding was traced to the exact CSS and JS behind it, reproduced on screen as evidence, and written so an engineer could act without a single follow-up question. There was also a timing dimension: campaigns and seasonal drops change the storefront weekly, so findings had to be written as reusable rules rather than one-off patches. And because paid traffic lands on deep pages — not just the homepage — every entry point, not only the happy path, had to hold up. The mandate went beyond polish: reshape the store creatively in Figma, then prove the reshape in revenue — CRO strategy and AOV-boosting patterns designed in from the first wireframe, not bolted on after launch.",
    investigation: "The storefront was walked end to end: 13 captured states across desktop and mobile, a recorded discovery flow, and targeted probes of the highest-risk moments — variant switching, cart handoff, and checkout-adjacent screens. Every state was compared against its siblings for claim and content agreement. Screenshots were taken in fixed sequences — arrive, scan, interact, hesitate, proceed — so the evidence would show not just what each screen looked like, but what a buyer would have been thinking at that exact moment.",
    rootCause: "The pattern across findings is state that doesn't survive journey steps and content that enters the site through more than one door. Typical of a catalogue that grew faster than its content rules — every issue found here is a rule waiting to be written, not a platform limitation. That distinction matters: rules are cheap to write, cheap to test, and they keep the next campaign from resurrecting the same defects under a new coat of paint.",
