    challenge: "Wells Industries's buyers don't tolerate guesswork: a texas-made custom furniture for hospitality spaces purchase is researched across multiple visits, on whatever device is at hand. The audit had to confirm the storefront keeps its promises consistently — pricing, availability, delivery — across every repeat visit and viewport, and that the checkout path stays intact from first click to confirmation. The commercial context raised the stakes further: margins in this category are won on repeat purchase, which makes any friction a compounding loss rather than a one-time leak. So the audit treated each defect not as a cosmetic flaw but as a measurable tax on acquisition spend. Every redesign decision answered to two numbers: conversion rate and average order value. Clearer screens to convert, smarter offers to grow the basket, and a checkout flow that never stalls between desire and payment.",
    investigation: "The storefront was walked end to end: 13 captured states across desktop and mobile, a recorded discovery flow, and targeted probes of the highest-risk moments — variant switching, cart handoff, and checkout-adjacent screens. Every state was compared against its siblings for claim and content agreement. Each state was captured twice, on separate passes, to separate real defects from transient glitches — anything that could not be reproduced twice was re-tested until it declared itself either way.",
    rootCause: "The pattern across findings is state that doesn't survive journey steps and content that enters the site through more than one door. Typical of a catalogue that grew faster than its content rules — every issue found here is a rule waiting to be written, not a platform limitation. Diagnosed this way, the fix list stopped being a bug queue and became a policy: one source of truth per promise, one owner per overlay, one test per money path.",
